# Insight Log — `fp8_mqa_logits` / `_fp8_mqa_logits_kernel` (Triton, gfx942 / MI300X-class)

Baseline geomean 5.5324 ms. Cumulative best after round 1: **0.8169 ms = 6.77x** (integrated
`r1_d0 + r1_d1`). Correctness: `err_ratio=0.0000`, `cos_diff=3.5e-09`, ALL PASSED.

---

## Round 1

| id | specialty | title | expected | verified | verdict |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| r1_d0 | compute | Native fnuz fp8 MFMA in the kernel dot | 3.5x | 2.7928x | partial (confirmed mechanism) |
| r1_d1 | host_runtime | Repair launcher tile heuristic + host-side waste | 1.9x | 2.0512x | confirmed |
| integrate | — | stacked r1_d0 + r1_d1 (clean `git apply`, no hand-merge) | — | **6.7551x** | super-multiplicative |

Winner: integrated patch,
`round_1/integrate/integrated_patch.diff`. Per-case 5.32 / 7.01 / 7.53 / 7.41x
(M=2048/4096/8000/8192); arithmetic 34.877 -> 4.762 ms = 7.32x.

Bottleneck shift: **compute(software fp8->fp16 dequant) -> compute(VALU tax around a native
MFMA)**. Inner KV loop 2570 instrs / 16 `v_mfma_f32_32x32x8_f16` -> 884 instrs / 64
`v_mfma_f32_16x16x32_fp8_fp8`. Occupancy 25% -> 37.5% (240 -> 168 VGPRs). 2.8% -> 16.5-21.7%
of fp8 MFMA peak.

---

## Insight blackboard (durable)

1. **The baseline was doing fp8 math in fp16.** The dot compiled to `v_mfma_f32_32x32x8_f16`;
   bitcasting the operands to `tl.float8e4b8` (fnuz, the gfx942-native format) emits
   `v_mfma_f32_16x16x32_fp8_fp8` and deletes ~97% of the inner loop. This single change is the
   whole 2.79x of r1_d0.
2. **The fnuz 0x80 NaN scrub is a correctness PREREQUISITE, not an optimization.** Without it
   `err_ratio` is exactly 0.1831 = the fraction of K columns containing >=1 `0x80` byte (375/2048);
   one NaN byte poisons its entire output column.
3. **The cheapest scrub is an int8 saturate, not compare+select.** Viewed as int8, `0x80` is
   `INT8_MIN`, so one `tl.maximum(x, -127)` neutralises it and is the identity on every other
   byte. In-loop cost 761 -> 634 instrs vs a compare/select form. `0x80 -> 0x81` turns a would-be
   zero into +-2^-9; measured max rel. error 1.9e-3, ~26x under the 5e-2 gate.
4. **Scrub placement beats scrub formulation: hoist it host-side.** `k_fp8` is `[N,D]`; the in-loop
   scrub re-scrubs the same bytes once per output row (M times). One
   `torch.clamp_min(k_fp8.view(torch.int8), -127).view(fp8)` in the launcher removes 258 instrs
   (29% of the loop). MEASURED 1.19x, correctness ALL PASSED (0.5673 -> 0.4848 ms at M=4096).
   A no-scrub-at-all probe runs 0.4758 ms, so the host hoist captures ~98% of this headroom —
   nothing left after it lands. This is the round-2 must-do.
5. **The LDS gate bug was a units error, not a budget problem.** `kv_bytes = head_size*BLOCK_KV*
   NUM_STAGES` put `NUM_STAGES` in the bytes-per-element slot, modelling a 16-bit KV tile, and so
   rejected `BLOCK_KV=128`. Measured `kernel.metadata.shared` for (128, ns=2, H=64, D=128) is
   **16896 B**, not 32768: `num_stages=2` does NOT double LDS here because the pipeliner keeps
   stage 2 in registers for a single-dot loop. `BLOCK_KV=256` is still correctly rejected (33280 B)
   and also measures slower.
6. **The two lanes COMPOUND, they do not overlap.** 2.79 x 2.05 = 5.73 predicted, 6.76 measured.
   Reason: the native fp8 MFMA halves the KV operand width, which is exactly what makes the
   corrected LDS model admit the 128-wide tile. Keeping the kernel-body lane strictly inside
   `@triton.jit` and the host lane strictly in the launcher gave a zero-conflict stack.
7. **`waves_per_eu=3` is worth ~6% and was not in the plan.** 2 / 3 / 4 -> 0.5855 / 0.5567 /
   0.5786 ms at M=4096; 4 is a cliff. Re-probed under the new fnuz kernel and still optimal.
8. **The kernel did NOT flip to latency-bound (C2) as round 0 predicted.** `waves_per_eu=4` is
   slower than 3 and `num_warps=8` is slower than 4 — the anti-signature of issue-wait. More
   resident waves do not help; fewer instructions do.
9. **The tuning search space is EXHAUSTED — do not re-sweep.** All five axes are strict interior
   optima with both neighbours worse: `BLOCK_KV` 64/128/256 = 0.873/**0.557**/1.137;
   `matrix_instr_nonkdim` 16/32 = **0.557**/0.706; `num_stages` 1/2/3/4 = 1.620/**1.583**/2.292/
   2.313; `num_warps` 1/2/4/8 = 10.75/2.89/**1.60**/3.01; `waves_per_eu` 2/3/4 = 0.586/**0.557**/
   0.579.
10. **No launch/overhead floor exists here.** Exactly 1 dispatch per call; latency scales linearly
    with work (78/138/244/250 ns per Melem) and the SMALL cases are cheaper per element — the
    opposite of an overhead-bound signature. HIP-graph capture / dispatch collapse is not a lever
    for this op. HBM at 148-215 GB/s = 2.8-4.1% of 5.3 TB/s, so not memory-bound either.
11. **Dropping the `torch.full(-inf)` prefill needs a KERNEL-side epilogue, not a host test.**
    The fill costs 8.7/20.4/55.9/52.0 us (~1.5-3% end-to-end, now a bigger share than at baseline),
    but any host-side "is the window full?" check needs a `.item()` sync costing 60-67 us flat —
    a net loss. `torch.empty` alone is 9.1 vs 27.2 us at M=4096, so the ceiling is ~18 us / ~3%.
12. **`input_precision="ieee"` is a no-op with native fp8 operands.** 0.5651 vs 0.5683 ms —
    inside noise. Closed.

## Confirmed dead-ends (do not re-explore)

- Transposing the dot to `[BLOCK_KV, NUM_HEADS]` so heads become the contiguous reduction axis:
  **2.4x REGRESSION** (3.923 vs 1.647 ms); `tl.trans` on fp8 operands forces a costly relayout.
- Replacing `tl.sum` with a cheaper cross-lane tree (probed via `tl.max`): slower (2.168 vs
  1.975 ms) — the DPP chain is already near the compiler's floor for this layout.
- Host-side "window already full" test to skip the `-inf` prefill: sync cost > fill cost.
- `matrix_instr_nonkdim=32`, `waves_per_eu=4`: both re-probed under the fnuz kernel, both regress.
- `input_precision="ieee"`: no measurable effect.

## Remaining headroom

- #4 host-side K scrub: measured **1.19x** -> geomean ~0.69 ms = **~8.0x**. Safe, verified.
- Cross-lane `tl.sum` over NUM_HEADS=64 (14% of the loop): bounded at ~1.10x, but both cheap
  reformulations already regressed. The only untried route is folding `w_block` into the MFMA
  (scale Q per-head before the dot) — blocked by the `relu` sitting between dot and head-sum, so
  it needs real algebra. Speculative.
- Kernel-side `-inf` epilogue replacing `torch.full`: ~1.5-3%, freebie only.
- Structural ceiling ~20-25% of fp8 MFMA peak: the op is a rank-128 dot plus a 64-way cross-lane
  reduction per 128 outputs, and the MFMA is only 7.2% of issued instruction slots.
